 352:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** /*
 353:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** ***************************************************************************************************
 354:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** *                                             FLUSH QUEUE
 355:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** *
 356:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** * Description : This function is used to flush the contents of the message queue.
 357:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** *
 358:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** * Arguments   : none
 359:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** *
 360:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** * Returns     : OS_ERR_NONE         upon success
 361:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** *               OS_ERR_EVENT_TYPE   If you didn't pass a pointer to a queue
 362:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** *               OS_ERR_PEVENT_NULL  If 'pevent' is a NULL pointer
 363:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** *
 364:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** * WARNING     : You should use this function with great care because, when to flush the queue, you 
 365:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** *               the references to what the queue entries are pointing to and thus, you could cause
 366:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** *               'memory leaks'.  In other words, the data you are pointing to that's being referenc
 367:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** *               by the queue entries should, most likely, need to be de-allocated (i.e. freed).
 368:./ucos/Core/os_q.c **** ***************************************************************************************************
